[{:db/id "q-334955/field-1/7da45e43d68636a1c0be98a4e08f8665107c0c9c41cca908ea82d06387391211" :answer/id #uuid "eeaf56b4-cffd-5eb1-8362-f76bbe26c288" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+e^t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334955/field-1/8a55a1e763e7d118b438f4c707fb4ff57c1696229fbfb038a8c1619ee8ba7e38" :answer/id #uuid "d10f68a2-4eb5-578e-8679-85e5048d40dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228218 :answer-field/correct "q-334955/field-1/7da45e43d68636a1c0be98a4e08f8665107c0c9c41cca908ea82d06387391211" :answer-field/choices ["q-334955/field-1/7da45e43d68636a1c0be98a4e08f8665107c0c9c41cca908ea82d06387391211" "q-334955/field-1/8a55a1e763e7d118b438f4c707fb4ff57c1696229fbfb038a8c1619ee8ba7e38"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-334955"] :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-334972/field-1/e512d27eefb0e113f1dd5eb50ab2d4951bac3c0842936a7b28f5f77455b2da05" :answer/id #uuid "35bc3e6f-9cff-5625-914d-d63da0c0876f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e^t(1+e^t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334972/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1acf0f02-d5d7-5137-8258-e64b60ec45dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334972/field-1/e512d27eefb0e113f1dd5eb50ab2d4951bac3c0842936a7b28f5f77455b2da05" :answer-field/choices ["q-334972/field-1/e512d27eefb0e113f1dd5eb50ab2d4951bac3c0842936a7b28f5f77455b2da05"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334972" :question/id #uuid "247239c4-d78f-5cb9-9da2-e8f3b6f91f3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334972" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334972/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334972"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334935/field-1/67a60dbf6a183a340b7d25522264a6c4ebe57fdfdb3386c0255a06b573d709e7" :answer/id #uuid "c960e103-028a-55c0-980a-65a14a754183" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14t^2+6t^5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334935/field-1/1312c5e5850a318380de2d77173df2fa33f834d34d73ce289cd6d0341c29bd21" :answer/id #uuid "0e3aa6a9-8e85-57ce-bbe3-e4405eabfbd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14t^2+6t^3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334935/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "8995fa53-c0a6-5562-809e-9325daeab34e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334935/field-1/67a60dbf6a183a340b7d25522264a6c4ebe57fdfdb3386c0255a06b573d709e7" :answer-field/choices ["q-334935/field-1/67a60dbf6a183a340b7d25522264a6c4ebe57fdfdb3386c0255a06b573d709e7" "q-334935/field-1/1312c5e5850a318380de2d77173df2fa33f834d34d73ce289cd6d0341c29bd21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334935" :question/id #uuid "91b9ca00-d2fa-58fd-b597-a00f0d7bbfc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-334935" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}6{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{1}^{2}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =6{t}^{2}(\\frac{7}{3}+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}(3{t}^{3}+7).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334935/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334935"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-334939/field-1/6afd566c6a368bbc1c8632b3a50568dc3931a4e95481e0df9f6692d9ad151868" :answer/id #uuid "76fa5adf-5662-5442-bbab-dca086b37914" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4t^3-3t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-334939/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "534cf067-0e36-5457-abd5-28af01fb8383" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-334939/field-1/6afd566c6a368bbc1c8632b3a50568dc3931a4e95481e0df9f6692d9ad151868" :answer-field/choices ["q-334939/field-1/6afd566c6a368bbc1c8632b3a50568dc3931a4e95481e0df9f6692d9ad151868"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-334939" :question/id #uuid "b4d61795-82bf-597c-8cfb-6a4059b19375" :question/math-academy-id "q-334939" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =4t(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}4t(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4t{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(3{x}^{3}+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-1}^{0}+{t}^{2}x{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =4t({t}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =t(4{t}^{2}-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-334939/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-334939"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-334667"] :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 1$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=x{e}^{tx}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂[1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t\\ge 1.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-335253/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "c9228306-67f7-58a7-a84d-778cd877070d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335253/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "d83f088f-4270-5678-a535-0acf67284a53" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335253/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "8dbe385d-c3bb-5ec4-9239-b8a851ea45ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335253/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "ad5bdec5-c184-5b33-a9b6-7e7a6a69bbdf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335253/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "e907054f-f9da-530b-bc60-b28719bbc9b1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335253/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7de5a58a-9f3b-5ea7-a3bb-16d32491d0da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335253/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-335253/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-335253/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-335253/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-335253/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-335253/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335253" :question/id #uuid "4b3c2f68-0bf9-5327-902b-2804c785b8b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-335253" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>-1$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ (since ${x}^{2}+{t}^{2}>0$ on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-\\frac{t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{3/2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xt).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ so that $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xt)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{1+t{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>-1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{(1+t{x}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>-1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335253/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335253"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-335255/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "3ad04d6b-e67d-53ab-9bb6-495c58dc60af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335255/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "68436ea9-29dc-503c-8837-3bb304c8884b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335255/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "4c24db0a-9d5b-51e8-afde-cb28c9532c21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335255/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "ce345467-4d76-5dd2-9f84-cce3be3e53d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335255/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "21696c8e-a852-5d01-8d44-af2154ac5b0b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335255/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3fa8349f-d5dd-5428-9870-0f644e840e63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335255/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-335255/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-335255/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-335255/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-335255/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-335255/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335255" :question/id #uuid "147a5eb9-ccf5-5722-a03e-4ab0930909aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-335255" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)={e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335255/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335255"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-335237"] :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={t}^{2}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=2tx\\operatorname{cos}⁡(tx)-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(tx)\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in R.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t<0$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ is positive, so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-335248/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "cf9cee0f-3114-5eb5-ae63-8a55700bcdde" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335248/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "befc73bf-d726-5984-b507-9dfcbdf24aea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335248/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "884f78c9-d44e-5f4e-b66a-eb347098e6d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335248/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "7a61897a-3dcf-5042-8a29-53119e41ae61" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335248/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "74869bbb-9b63-5446-9f6d-4247153196f6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-335248/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7cffdb50-c759-5ab2-8974-4258e8b2b319" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-335248/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-335248/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-335248/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-335248/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-335248/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-335248/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-335248" :question/id #uuid "baf1d0ef-7b1b-5759-935f-46c2ba418805" :question/math-academy-id "q-335248" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{|x-t|}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $0<c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{-xt}-{x}^{3}t{e}^{-xt}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{|x-t|}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-335248/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-335248"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
